WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.910
<v ->Now let's talk about AWS Compute Optimizer,</v>

2
00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:05.730
which is used to reduce cost and improve performance

3
00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:09.360
by recommending optimal AWS resources for your workload.

4
00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:13.408
So this is going to do an analysis of your EC2 instances,

5
00:00:13.408 --> 00:00:15.630
your autoscaling groups, and tell you, for example,

6
00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:18.330
which one are over provisioned or under provisioned.

7
00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:20.610
And then you can build your optimizations.

8
00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:22.440
And then you can have a better cost perspective,

9
00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:23.970
and also a better performance.

10
00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:25.950
This way it does it is that it will use machine learning

11
00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:28.650
under the hood to analyze your resource configuration,

12
00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:30.780
as well as track their CloudWatch metrics

13
00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:32.790
to understand their utilization.

14
00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:34.830
So supported resources by Compute Optimizer

15
00:00:34.830 --> 00:00:37.530
are EC2 instances, auto scanning groups,

16
00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:39.870
EBS volumes, Lambda functions.

17
00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:41.640
And this allows you to lower your cost

18
00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:44.280
by up to 25% without doing much.

19
00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:46.357
And the recommendations themselves

20
00:00:46.357 --> 00:00:48.300
can be exported into Amazon S3

21
00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:52.110
In order to get the most out of Compute Optimizer,

22
00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:55.230
and if you want to analyze also the memory utilization

23
00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:58.200
and so therefore get recommendations on RAM,

24
00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:01.200
then you need to install the CloudWatch Agents.

25
00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:03.810
So therefore, you have your EC2 instances.

26
00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:05.730
And they're running the CloudWatch agents.

27
00:01:05.730 --> 00:01:07.620
And then they will send their metrics,

28
00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:10.770
including the Ram, to CloudWatch service itself.

29
00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:13.830
And then Compute Optimizer can analyze these metrics

30
00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:16.920
out of CloudWatch and make recommendations as well

31
00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:20.820
for the Compute Optimizer on the RAM, on the memory.

32
00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:24.030
But just so you know, this CloudWatch Agent is not needed

33
00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:27.240
if you analyze the CPU or the NetworkIn/out

34
00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:29.283
or the DiskReadOps and the DiskWriteOps.

35
00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:31.830
So that's it for this lecture,

36
00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:35.013
I hope you liked it, and I will see you in the next lecture.

